"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 4.3 and an ISUP grade group 2 (Gleason 3+4). Positive surgical margins are noted, which elevates concern. Conversely, the absence of extraprostatic extension, seminal vesicle invasion, and lymphovascular invasion are favorable findings. The lack of nodal sampling means that pNx status is documented, widening uncertainty regarding nodal involvement. Biopsy-to-prostatectomy differences cannot be assessed as the biopsy Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing. Prognostic revision would significantly benefit from postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory.\n\nThe current data suggests an intermediate risk profile based on available metrics. The absence of follow-up information limits definitive prognostic statements regarding recurrence risk beyond the provided horizon estimate. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 41.64–97.15 months; its coverage has not been validated."
